              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The physical feature we call the \"Adam's apple\" is actually the **laryngeal prominence**\u2014a protrusion formed by the thyroid cartilage, which acts as a protective shield around the larynx (voice box). \n\nWhile both men and women have this cartilage, men typically have much larger and more visible Adam\u2019s apples. This difference comes down to three main factors: **hormones, the size of the vocal cords, and the angle of the neck cartilage.**\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Testosterone Surge at Puberty\nBefore puberty, boys and girls have similarly sized laryngeal structures and high-pitched voices. \n\nHowever, during male puberty, a sharp rise in **testosterone** signals the body to undergo significant structural changes. This hormone stimulates the larynx and the thyroid cartilage to grow rapidly and expand. In females, puberty is driven by estrogen, which does not stimulate the same dramatic level of cartilage and larynx growth.\n\n### 2. The Angle of the Cartilage\nThe thyroid cartilage is made of two plates (laminae) that meet at the front of your throat like the bow of a ship. The angle at which these two plates join determines how much the bump protrudes:\n* **In Men:** Because the larynx grows so large, the two cartilage plates meet at a sharp, acute angle\u2014typically **around 90 degrees** (and sometimes even sharper). This creates a sharp, highly visible point.\n* **In Women:** Because the larynx grows very little, the plates meet at a much wider, flatter angle\u2014typically **around 120 degrees**. This wide curve lies flat against the neck and rarely creates a visible bump.\n\n### 3. Accommodating Deeper Voices\nThe size of your Adam's apple is directly linked to the pitch of your voice. Inside the larynx are your vocal cords. \nAs a male's larynx grows under the influence of testosterone, his vocal cords grow significantly **longer and thicker** (similar to the thick strings on a bass guitar). To accommodate these longer cords, the thyroid cartilage must tip forward and expand, pushing the front of the throat outward. This is why men have deeper voices and a much more prominent \"bump\". \n\n### Do women have an Adam's apple?\nYes, **everyone has one**, but in women, it is usually not visible. Because the female larynx is smaller and the cartilage angle is wider, it remains hidden. Additionally, women naturally tend to have a slightly higher percentage of subcutaneous fat in their neck area, which further cushions and conceals the thyroid cartilage. However, some women with naturally larger laryngeal structures or lower body fat percentages may have a visible Adam's apple as well.",
